In a discussion, James inquired about using an exponential function to approximate annual discounting. I confirmed that it is a common practice in finance and economics, facilitating the representation of compound effects over time. I provided the exponential discounting formula for both discrete and continuous compounding, detailing components like present value (PV), future value (FV), annual discount rate (r), and time (t). I emphasized that the model assumes a constant discount rate and highlighted its utility in simplifying the complexities of compounding while cautioning about assumptions regarding interest rates over long periods.
